{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"REVISED again. Round 2: 7 blocks, ALL THE SAME FINDING — and it is the worst instance yet of my\nrecurring failure, because I introduced the contradiction WHILE FIXING the very thing it contradicts.\n\nRound 1 told me my sync.remote assertion was a bug. I corrected the reasoning in the Approach body\n('Do NOT assert on sync.remote — it defaults to origin regardless') and then left TWO stale statements\nsaying the opposite: the isolation-assertion sentence in layer 4, and AC bullet 2 ('an empty\nsync.remote is asserted'). So the plan simultaneously forbade and mandated the same check. An\nimplementer following the ACs would have written exactly the broken assertion round 1 caught.\n\nSIXTH INSTANCE of the same shape this session: J9's Scope file list (twice), J9's observability\nmechanism, J8's Approach omissions, J8's AC member count, and now this. The pattern is precise: I\nrevise the ANALYSIS and leave the CRITERIA, or revise the criteria and leave Scope. Every one caught\nby the gate; none by me.\n\nThe habit that would have caught it takes ten seconds: after editing a plan, grep the whole document\nfor the term I just changed and read every hit. I did not do that here even though round 1's finding\nnamed the exact term. Adopting it as a standing step.\n\nFixed: the no-remote check is now the single stated assertion, in both the Approach and the ACs, with\nthe reason inline so nobody re-adds the sync.remote form. Re-review running."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785512572895442001,"uuid":"f89a6297-62b6-4b35-bb28-6ad5be9067b0"}